GFG — Bottom View of Binary Tree
Problem Understanding

The bottom view of a binary tree contains the nodes that are visible when the tree is viewed from the bottom.

The key concept is Horizontal Distance (HD).

For every node:

root          → HD = 0
left child    → HD - 1
right child   → HD + 1

If multiple nodes have the same horizontal distance, the bottom-most/deepest node is visible.

Finally, answer must be returned from:

smallest HD → largest HD
Example

Consider:

                 20
               /    \
              8      22
             / \      \
            5   3      25
               / \
              10 14

Horizontal distances:

                20(0)
              /      \
          8(-1)       22(1)
          /   \         \
      5(-2)  3(0)      25(2)
             /   \
         10(-1)  14(1)

For each HD:

HD = -2 → 5
HD = -1 → 10   ← replaces 8
HD =  0 → 3    ← replaces 20
HD =  1 → 14   ← replaces 22
HD =  2 → 25

Therefore:

Bottom View = [5, 10, 3, 14, 25]
The Core Pattern

This problem is essentially:

Tree
 ↓
Assign Horizontal Distance
 ↓
For each HD:
    keep the deepest node
 ↓
Sort HD from left → right

Compare with related problems:

Top View
→ first node at each HD

Bottom View
→ last/deepest node at each HD

Approach 1 — Brute Force
Idea

For every possible horizontal distance:

Traverse the entire tree.
Find the deepest node having that HD.
Put its value into the answer.

This means we repeatedly scan the tree.

Step 1: Find Minimum and Maximum HD

For example:

                 20
               /    \
              8      22
             / \      \
            5   3      25
               / \
              10 14

HD range is:

-2 → 2

Then for every HD:

-2
-1
 0
 1
 2

we traverse the entire tree to find the deepest node.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    int minHD;
    int maxHD;

    static class Result {
        int value;
        int depth;

        Result(int value, int depth) {
            this.value = value;
            this.depth = depth;
        }
    }

    boolean isLeaf(Node root) {
        return root.left == null && root.right == null;
    }

    void findRange(Node root, int hd) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        minHD = Math.min(minHD, hd);
        maxHD = Math.max(maxHD, hd);

        findRange(root.left, hd - 1);
        findRange(root.right, hd + 1);
    }

    void findDeepest(
            Node root,
            int hd,
            int depth,
            int targetHD,
            Result result) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (hd == targetHD) {

            if (depth >= result.depth) {
                result.depth = depth;
                result.value = root.data;
            }
        }

        findDeepest(root.left, hd - 1, depth + 1, targetHD, result);
        findDeepest(root.right, hd + 1, depth + 1, targetHD, result);
    }

    public ArrayList<Integer> bottomView(Node root) {

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        minHD = 0;
        maxHD = 0;

        // Find HD range
        findRange(root, 0);

        // For every HD, search the whole tree
        for (int hd = minHD; hd <= maxHD; hd++) {

            Result result = new Result(-1, -1);

            findDeepest(root, 0, 0, hd, result);

            ans.add(result.value);
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

