Approach 2 — DFS + Map

Now instead of repeatedly traversing the tree, traverse it only once.

For each node, store:

HD → (deepest depth, node value)

For example:

HD = -1
→ depth = 3
→ value = 10

Whenever we find a node at the same HD:

if currentDepth >= storedDepth

replace it.

The >= is useful because if two nodes have the same depth and HD, the later left-to-right DFS node becomes the stored value.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    static class Info {
        int depth;
        int value;

        Info(int depth, int value) {
            this.depth = depth;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    void dfs(
            Node root,
            int hd,
            int depth,
            TreeMap<Integer, Info> map) {

        if (root == null) {
            return;
        }

        if (!map.containsKey(hd)
                || depth >= map.get(hd).depth) {

            map.put(hd, new Info(depth, root.data));
        }

        dfs(root.left, hd - 1, depth + 1, map);
        dfs(root.right, hd + 1, depth + 1, map);
    }

    public ArrayList<Integer> bottomView(Node root) {

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        TreeMap<Integer, Info> map = new TreeMap<>();

        dfs(root, 0, 0, map);

        for (Info info : map.values()) {
            ans.add(info.value);
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

