Approach 4 — BFS + HashMap + HD Range

Now we can optimize the TreeMap.

We don't actually need a balanced tree to sort the keys.

Observe:

If there are n nodes,
HD is always between -n and +n.

We can therefore use:

HashMap
+
minimum HD
+
maximum HD

Then at the end iterate:

minHD → maxHD

Since the number of possible HD positions is at most O(n), this gives O(n) average time.

This is asymptotically better than TreeMap.

Code
import java.util.*;

class Pair {
    Node node;
    int hd;
    int depth;

    Pair(Node node, int hd, int depth) {
        this.node = node;
        this.hd = hd;
        this.depth = depth;
    }
}

class Info {
    int depth;
    int value;

    Info(int depth, int value) {
        this.depth = depth;
        this.value = value;
    }
}

class Solution {

    public ArrayList<Integer> bottomView(Node root) {

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null) {
            return ans;
        }

        HashMap<Integer, Info> map = new HashMap<>();

        Queue<Pair> q = new LinkedList<>();

        q.offer(new Pair(root, 0, 0));

        int minHD = 0;
        int maxHD = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            Pair curr = q.poll();

            Node node = curr.node;
            int hd = curr.hd;
            int depth = curr.depth;

            minHD = Math.min(minHD, hd);
            maxHD = Math.max(maxHD, hd);

            if (!map.containsKey(hd)
                    || depth >= map.get(hd).depth) {

                map.put(hd, new Info(depth, node.data));
            }

            if (node.left != null) {
                q.offer(
                    new Pair(node.left, hd - 1, depth + 1)
                );
            }

            if (node.right != null) {
                q.offer(
                    new Pair(node.right, hd + 1, depth + 1)
                );
            }
        }

        for (int hd = minHD; hd <= maxHD; hd++) {

            if (map.containsKey(hd)) {
                ans.add(map.get(hd).value);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

Why Do We Keep depth in Approach 4?

Your original code does not need depth because BFS processes nodes by levels.

But after replacing TreeMap with HashMap, it's cleaner and safer to explicitly store:

HD
depth
value

Then our rule becomes:

if (depth >= oldDepth)

replace the current value.

Thus the logic is explicit rather than relying completely on traversal order.

